{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgShbE8pYArLxZKQhtuLP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Your Friday in St. Louis (Oct 30)\n\nIt's Halloween eve, so the city will be lively.\n\n**Late morning: The Griot Museum of Black History** (Old North St. Louis)\nA small, deeply personal museum with life-size figures of Black Missourians most people never learned about. It's a real local gem. Check the hours, since they're limited.\n\n**Lunch: Smoki O's** (North Broadway)\nGet the rib tips and a **snoot**, crispy fried pig snout, a true St. Louis soul food staple most visitors never hear of. It runs on lunch hours, so go early.\n\n**Afternoon: A slow drive through The Ville**\nPass **Sumner High School**, the first Black high school west of the Mississippi. Tina Turner, Chuck Berry, Arthur Ashe and **Dick Gregory**, the original king of comedy, all went there. Annie Malone's Poro College empire was also in this neighborhood.\n\n**Evening: Comedy**\nCheck **Helium** or **The Funny Bone** for Halloween-weekend headliners. It's a big weekend for touring Black comics. Cedric the Entertainer came up in north county, and this city takes its comedy seriously.\n\n**Late night: Find a steppers set**\nSt. Louis has a real steppin' scene. Search for a grown-folks R&B or steppers night this Friday. If they don't close with \"Before I Let Go,\" you're in the wrong room.\n\n**Last stop: A St. Paul sandwich**\nAn egg foo young patty on white bread with pickles and mayo, from any corner Chinese takeout. It's peak St. Louis, and outsiders have never heard of it.\n\n*Double-check hours before heading out, since small spots change often.*\n\nWant me to tune this for a budget, a date, or a group?"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":647,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_